.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"},{"content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"discussion","text":"Discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Thick-restart Lanczos (Wu–Simon) with full reorthogonalization and locking on σI − M (σ","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Gershgorin’s bound), from a fixed start vector; once k pairs are found, a run on the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"complement of their vectors checks that no copy of a multiple eigenvalue was missed. A Ritz","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pair is accepted when its residual is at most ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tolerance"},{"text":" · max(|λ|, s\/1000), s Gershgorin’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"bound on the spectrum; the vectors are then rotated by Rayleigh–Ritz on M, whose values are","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"returned. The dense solver below 64 vertices, or when k is a third of them or more.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"O((n + m) · products + n · k²) time, O(n · k) ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Double"},{"text":"s for the vectors and n · max(30, 2k +","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"10) for the basis.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"weight"},{"type":"text","text":" gives the weight of the edge at each position, read once per position; a weight of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"0 is no edge."}]},{"type":"aside","name":"Precondition","style":"note","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":""},{"code":"smallest","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is in "},{"code":"0...vertexCount","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":"tolerance > 0","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", every weight is finite"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and nonnegative, and "},{"code":"maxIterations","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", when given, is at least 1."}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"type":"aside","name":"Throws","style":"note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"NoConvergence"},{"text":" when a Lanczos run spends ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"maxIterations"},{"text":" products without","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"converging, with the products spent and the largest Ritz residual on the tolerance’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"scale. The dense solver throws as ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"adjacencySpectrum"},{"text":" does.","type":"text"}]}]}],"kind":"content"}],"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/spectralgraphtheory\/graphprotocols\/graph\/signlesslaplacianeigenpairs(smallest:weight:tolerance:maxiterations:)-6utep"]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/SpectralGraphTheory\/documentation\/SpectralGraphTheory\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/signlessLaplacianEigenpairs(smallest:weight:tolerance:maxIterations:)-6utep","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"references":{"doc://SpectralGraphTheory/documentation/SpectralGraphTheory/Eigenpairs":{"type":"topic","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Eigenpairs"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"Eigenpairs"}],"url":"\/documentation\/spectralgraphtheory\/eigenpairs","title":"Eigenpairs","identifier":"doc:\/\/SpectralGraphTheory\/documentation\/SpectralGraphTheory\/Eigenpairs","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"Eigenpairs of a graph matrix at one end of its spectrum: the eigenvalues, from that end","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(ascending for the smallest, descending for the largest), and an orthonormal eigenvector for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"te